from h2 import *
A = dict(id="A", levels=L2, kind="Web page", source="Wayfarer Travel Guides", numbered=True, title="Planning an independent trip", body=[
 {"p": "Travelling independently means choosing your own route, your own pace and your own surprises. Israel is small enough to cross in a day, yet varied enough to fill several weeks, with beaches, deserts, mountains and ancient cities all within a few hours of each other."},
 {"p": "Getting around is easier than many visitors expect. Trains run along the coast and between the larger cities, and buses reach most towns and villages. Shared taxis, which follow set routes and leave when full, are a cheap way to cover short distances. It is worth buying a rechargeable travel card as soon as you arrive."},
 {"p": "There is a place to stay for every budget. Hostels are popular with younger travellers, while family-run guesthouses offer a warm welcome and a home-cooked breakfast. Some kibbutz guesthouses, set among orchards and fields, are surprisingly affordable. Book your first night in advance, then see where the road takes you."},
 {"p": "Food is half the adventure. Markets are the best place to start: you can sample fresh bread, olives and spices before you buy, and a plate of hummus with warm pitta rarely costs much. Do not be shy about asking what is in a dish, and always give the local shakshuka a go."},
 {"p": "Be prepared for the weather. Summers are very hot, so carry water, wear a hat and plan long walks for early morning. Winters are mild but can be wet, especially in the north, so pack a light waterproof as well."},
 {"p": "Finally, respect local customs. Many businesses close from Friday afternoon until Saturday evening for Shabbat, so shop for supplies early, and dress modestly when you visit places of worship. A friendly greeting in Hebrew costs nothing, and you will be amazed at how far it goes."}])
B = dict(id="B", levels=L2, kind="Magazine article", source="Slow Journeys magazine", title="The rise of slow travel", body=[
 {"p": "More travellers are rejecting the whirlwind tour in favour of a gentler pace. A survey of 2,000 holidaymakers by the Slow Journeys Panel found that 62 per cent now prefer to stay in fewer places for longer, and more than half said they wanted a trip that left a lighter footprint on the places they visited. Travel companies are taking notice, and more itineraries now include free days and local homestays."},
 {"p": "Slow travel is not simply about moving less. It is about spending time in markets, cafés and small villages, which offer \"a window into everyday life\" that no guidebook can match. In Israel, where a morning's drive can take you from a Mediterranean beach to a desert canyon, that approach can transform a trip, because there is always something new within reach."},
 {"p": "Walkers are leading the way. Long-distance trails attract growing numbers each year, and many follow routes \"off the beaten track\", passing through farmland, forests and quiet communities that few tourists ever see. Cyclists and campers are following close behind, pitching their tents beside streams and hilltop viewpoints."},
 {"p": "Of course, there are difficulties. Taking more time costs more money, and some travellers find language barriers daunting. Others struggle to plan a flexible route, particularly in summer, when daytime temperatures can make walking uncomfortable. A few simply worry that they will miss the famous sights, and return home feeling that they have not done enough."},
 {"box": "**Advice from the Travel Better Network:** Learn a few words of the local language. Book your first night, but leave the rest open. Use public transport whenever you can. And remember: slow travel is about quality, not quantity."},
 {"p": "The Network also runs a free phrase-sharing website, where experienced travellers post tips on everything from bargaining in markets to finding the best walking routes. It has attracted thousands of members in its first year."},
 {"p": "In my opinion, slow travel is the best way to see any country, and every traveller should try it at least once. If you have ever returned from a holiday feeling more tired than when you left, this may be the answer."}])
C = dict(id="C", levels=L2, kind="Transcript of a talk", source="Wanderlust Travel Fair", title="Talk given at a travel fair", body=[
 {"p": "Good afternoon, everyone, and thank you for coming. I have been travelling around Israel for eleven years, and I still find something new on every trip. Today I want to convince you to put down the itinerary, pick up a rucksack and go."},
 {"p": "My first visit was a disaster, and I loved every minute of it. I missed my train, lost my map and ended up in a village I had never heard of. An old man invited me for coffee, and three hours later I knew his whole family story. That is when I realised that the best moments are never on the schedule."},
 {"p": "So what do you really need? First, you need curiosity. Ask questions, taste everything and talk to strangers. Second, you need flexibility, because buses are late and plans change. Third, you need patience, because the best stories take time to unfold."},
 {"p": "Will it always be comfortable? Honestly, no. You will be hot, hungry and lost at least once. But where else could you float in the Dead Sea at breakfast, eat the best hummus of your life at lunch, and watch the sun set over the desert before dinner?"},
 {"p": "The numbers back me up. Last year, more than forty thousand people took part in our walking festival, and nine out of ten said they would come back. That is not bad for an event that began with twelve friends and a single map."},
 {"p": "People often ask where to stay. My answer is simple: wherever you are welcome. I have slept in hostels, in a tent under the stars, in a kibbutz guesthouse among the orange groves and, once, on a friend's sofa in Haifa. Every one of those places taught me something."},
 {"p": "I have also learned to be spontaneous. If someone says, \"Come with us,\" say yes. If the path looks interesting, take it. If the market smells wonderful, follow your nose. Nobody ever regretted saying yes to a good adventure, and I have never met a traveller who wished they had planned more."},
 {"p": "You will find it exhausting, funny, humbling and unforgettable. Not every day will go to plan, but every day will be yours. So take a deep breath, buy the ticket and go. Thank you."}])

